# fixedbugs, defect 2877, 2026-11-27, lane win-longname (M-rebel-library step
# 212e).
#
# SYMPTOM: on Windows a program reaching a module named in 180 letters, as
# `fixedbugs2877/<180 letters>` is, did not compile from the CI's checkout,
# `D:\a\heroes\heroes`: *cannot write build/tu-<16>/<module>-<16>.o.key.txt:
# the operating system's own reason is 3*, exit 2, while the module's own
# source, 235 characters there, was read. Measured on the Windows box from a
# folder of the CI's 18 characters, a root module named in 180 `m`s built and
# one named in 200 was refused; from the 22 of the box's own, 180 was refused.
#
# CAUSE: every file of a module's unit under `build/tu-<key>/` was named by
# the module, `<module>.c`, `<module>.deps.txt`, `<module>-<sum>.o` and the
# object's seal `<module>-<sum>.o.key.txt`, each first under a private name
# about 12 characters longer, and Windows refuses a whole path past 259.
#
# FIX: each is named by the module's stem, `unit-` and sixteen hexadecimal
# digits of the module's digest (`selfhost/cli/unit_names.hero`), 48
# characters at the longest whatever the module's length; a link's refusal is
# placed at the module through the same stem (the `unsupported` case of the
# same defect). The module's source bounds the folder this case builds from:
# on Windows it takes 216 characters under the checkout, so a folder of up to
# 43 characters with its separator, where the CI's takes 19.
use fixedbugs2877/amodulewhosenametakesonehundredandeightylettershasitsunitsfilesnamedbyadigestofitsnameandnotbythenameitselfsotheyfitwhatawindowspathtakesfromthecheckoutsfolderandfromdeeperonesalso as long

function main()
    print(long.greeting())
    print(long.length())
